<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banu Sulaym</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Banu Sulaym were an Arab tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> who, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a>, migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in the 11th century, encouraged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> to punish the rebellious Zirids. Settling mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, Tripolitania and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, they fought as allies and rivals of the region's rulers, including the Banu Ghaniya, and spread Arabic speech and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> ways of life across the steppe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banu-sulaym.jpg" alt="Banu Sulaym" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MWahaiibii · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banu_Sulaym_Liwa%27_(35).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to Arab genealogical tradition the Banu Sulaym descended from Sulaym ibn Mansur, which made them part of the wider grouping of Qays Aylan. They formed three main divisions, Imru' al-Qays, Harith and Tha'laba, founded by sons or grandsons of Sulaym. Their homeland was the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, with the Harrah volcanic field, once named Harrat Bani Sulaym after them, at its heart: a region enemy horsemen could not manage, where the tribe kept protected pastures and the Bahz branch owned gold mines.</p><p>The Sulaym kept close ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and sold horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, sheep and clarified butter in the markets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, where they mediated between clans of the Banu Aws. They worshipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khami">Khamis</a>, an idol shared with the Banu Khazraj.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, the Sulaym were at first hostile to Muhammad. Several of their clans joined the attack on Muslim missionaries at Bi'r Ma'una in 625, and they fought beside the Quraysh at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trench">Battle of the Trench</a> in 627, but by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conquest-of-mecca">conquest of Mecca</a> in January 630 most had converted and joined him, and they fought for him at the Battle of Hunayn. Most defected from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> after his death in 632, yet after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ridda-wars">Ridda Wars</a> they took part in the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p><p>Most of the tribe backed Mu'awiya in the First Muslim Civil War. As members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> they recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, lost about 600 men at the Battle of Marj Rahit in 684, and in 686, under Umayr ibn al-Hubab, deserted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> at the Battle of Khazir. A war with the Taghlib along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur River</a> followed, in which Umayr was killed in 689.</p><p>From the late 9th century the Harb tribe from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> absorbed much of the Sulaym of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. Those in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz">al-Aziz</a> expelled them with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>. By the mid-12th century the Sulaym in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> had driven the Hilal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, and in the late 12th century those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharaf-al-din-qaraqush">Sharaf al-Din Qaraqush</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-ghaniya">Ali ibn Ishaq ibn Ghaniya</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Descendants of the Sulaym, known as the Sa'ada, are dominant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> today, and many of the region's Arab tribes trace their descent to them. The Sa'ada are divided into two groups: the Harabi, among them the Ubaydat and Bara'asa, and the Jabarina, among them the 'Awaqir and Magharba.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Banu Sulaym” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hafsid Ifriqiya combined an urban, literate society on the coast with Arab and Berber tribal groups in the steppe and the interior, many descended from the Banu Hilal and <b>Banu Sulaym</b> who had entered the region in the eleventh century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Banu Ghaniya fought as a roaming power, allied with Arab tribes such as the <b>Banu Sulaym</b> and, for a time, with Qaraqush.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-ghaniya-of-ifriqiya">Banu Ghaniya of Ifriqiya</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 750s the Abbasids resettled and refortified it as a key fortress of the thughur, the frontier zone facing Byzantium, and over time its governors, drawn from the local Arab tribe of <b>Sulaym</b>, became a hereditary dynasty only loosely bound to Baghdad.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-melitene">Emirate of Melitene</a></li><li><blockquote>In revenge the Fatimids encouraged the Banu Hilal and <b>Banu Sulaym</b> Arab tribes to move west.</blockquote><a href="/state/zirid-emirate">Zirid emirate</a></li></ul></section>
